{
  "skillId": "index-log-conversion",
  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
    "intro": "The two statements $a^{x}=y$ and $\\log_{a}y=x$ carry exactly the same information written two ways. Converting between them is a single move: find the base, the index and the number, then place them into the other layout. The same move works when the index is negative or fractional, because a logarithm can take any index as its value.",
    "facts": [
      "**The equivalence.** $a^{x}=y$ is equivalent to $\\log_{a}y=x$. The base $a$ stays the base, the index $x$ becomes the value of the logarithm, and the number $y$ becomes the argument.",
      "**Index to log.** From $5^{3}=125$ the base $5$ stays as the base, the argument is $125$ and the value is $3$, giving $\\log_{5}125=3$.",
      "**Log to index.** From $\\log_{10}10000=4$ the base $10$ is raised to the value $4$ to give the argument $10000$, so $10^{4}=10000$.",
      "**Negative indices carry their sign.** $10^{-2}=\\frac{1}{100}$ becomes $\\log_{10}\\frac{1}{100}=-2$; the index, including the minus sign, becomes the value.",
      "**Fractional indices work the same way.** $16^{\\frac{1}{2}}=4$ becomes $\\log_{16}4=\\frac{1}{2}$, and a root such as $\\sqrt{5}=5^{\\frac{1}{2}}$ can be converted once it is written as a fractional index.",
      "**A zero index.** $7^{0}=1$ becomes $\\log_{7}1=0$, because every base raised to the power $0$ is $1$."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Identify the base, the index and the number in the statement.",
      "Write the equivalent statement using $a^{x}=y \\Leftrightarrow \\log_{a}y=x$."
    ]
  },
